module extdeps.curl.exit_status

import std.types { String, Int }

data curl_exit_status_authority_note: String = "curl(1) EXIT CODES, cited to the upstream manual. Only the codes this repository actually reads are modelled; the list is deliberately not exhaustive, because a code nobody consumes is a row with no consumer."

// EXIT 7 IS THE ONE CODE THAT PROVES NOTHING IS LISTENING, and the distinction is the reason this
// row exists rather than a nonzero test at the call site. curl(1) documents 7 as "Failed to connect
// to host" -- the transport-level refusal a dial gets when no process holds the port. Every other
// nonzero says something else entirely: 6 could not resolve the host, 22 the server answered with an
// HTTP error, 28 the operation timed out. A caller that reads "nonzero" as "absent" therefore reads
// a DNS failure, an authentication rejection and a slow network as proof that a service is gone.
// Those other codes are named here in prose rather than minted as rows, because nothing reads them.
//
// Cited to curl(1) EXIT CODES, curl 8.x. The version is not pinned here because this code is stable
// across every curl release that has had it and the repository consumes no others; a row that grows
// to codes whose meaning has moved should carry the version it was read from.
data curl_exit_failed_to_connect: Int = 7
